#!/command/with-contenv /bin/bash
# fauna-iroh-relay sidecar — the nest's self-hosted P2P relay (no n0 third party).
# Always up: the image runs the relay, and nothing — no person, no RPC, no flag
# file — switches it on (p2p.md § The relay). Whether it SERVES is the nest's
# answer, not this script's: the relay dials nest's /internal/relay/ws, runs the
# fauna.sidecar.hello token handshake ATTESTING its X25519, and holds that channel
# for its whole life. Until the nest has a public name of its own it is handed no
# certificate and stands by, listening on loopback with nothing to present; from
# then on it fetches its relay.<domain> TLS cert as an HPKE-sealed blob it opens
# with that X25519 (NEVER /data/acme — security.md § UID isolation) and serves the
# iroh relay protocol over HTTPS on a loopback port the sni-router fronts at :443
# (SNI relay.<domain>). Every endpoint that connects is put to the nest over the
# same channel (fauna.relay.admit): only a member's device is served.
#
# Runs as the non-root fauna-relay UID (s6-setuidgid): its only /data access is its
# own 0700 /data/keys/relay (the X25519 keypair the entrypoint chowns to it). No
# setcap — it binds only loopback ports >1024.
#
# FAUNA_SIDECAR_TOKEN: the boot-minted per-sidecar token (sidecar_tokens.rs writes
# /data/sidecar-token-relay, mode 0600 fauna-owned). This run-script reads it as
# ROOT and passes it via `env` BEFORE dropping to fauna-relay (the file→env
# pattern); the relay UID itself never reads the token file.
#
# ⚠ That UID isolation is exactly why the value can only be a START-TIME snapshot:
# the relay cannot re-read the file to refresh it, and nest re-mints every sidecar
# token on EVERY boot. So the binary EXITS (78, EX_CONFIG) when nest refuses its
# `fauna.sidecar.hello` — on any dial of its channel, which otherwise leaves the
# relay admitting nobody and serving a cert it can never renew — and s6's
# restart re-runs this `cat` against nest's current token. Nest merely being
# unreachable is NOT a refusal (retried in-process), so the relay starting before
# nest is up does not restart-loop. Owner: transport.md § Future directions → the
# sidecar channel's credential lifetime.
#
# Ports: --https-bind 127.0.0.1:8445 is what the sni-router L4-splices SNI
# relay.<domain> to (WITHOUT a --send-proxy-to: iroh-relay does not parse PROXY v2,
# and the relay rate-limits per node-key not source IP, so losing the L4 client IP
# is fine — D.1). --http-bind 127.0.0.1:8446 (the relay's plaintext captive-portal
# probe) stays loopback-only — it is NOT SNI-routed, so it is not a public exposure.
#
# --nest-url: the loopback BASE; the dialer derives wss://…/internal/relay/ws and
# dials it (accepting the served self-signed cert via the capturing verifier).
# ALWAYS https:// — nest serves HTTPS on its bind port from boot via its always-live
# self-signed floor cert, regardless of domain (main.rs `prepare_listener_tls`;
# domainless-boot migration retired the `FAUNA_DOMAIN`-keyed http/https branch this
# script used to carry). Only the test/diagnostic `FAUNA_INSECURE_DISABLE_TLS` escape
# serves plain HTTP, and no deployment path ever sets it. (No s6
# dependencies.d/fauna-nest: the relay's own fetch-with-retry loop tolerates nest not
# yet being up.)
TOKEN=$(cat /data/sidecar-token-relay 2>/dev/null || true)
exec env FAUNA_SIDECAR_TOKEN="$TOKEN" \
    s6-setuidgid fauna-relay \
    fauna-iroh-relay \
    --https-bind 127.0.0.1:8445 \
    --http-bind 127.0.0.1:8446 \
    --nest-url "https://127.0.0.1:${FAUNA_PORT:-3000}" \
    --key-dir /data/keys/relay
